					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/orikae" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(438099); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=438099&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607245026" alt="orikae" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=438099)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/orikae" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(438099); return false;" >orikae</a></span> <span class="odate time_1307216493 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">04 Jun 2011 19:41</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1169826">
									

<p>I don't really understand the main thrust of the story.</p>
<p>For me, there's not enough build-up before the turn in the story, meaning that it comes off as silly rather than frightening or thought-provoking. Bright's 001 proposal works because of the layers of deceit woven into the narrative, and the factory itself having a storied history. Here, it's two old dudes in a room talking to each other, and there's not a lot of characterization for either. The story just doesn't work for me. Like jokes, horror needs to be introduced with some background in order for us to be surprised by the punchline - dropping us in medias res to the final confrontation robs the story of a lot of its potential power.</p>
<p>(doing my best to be constructive.)</p>

							</div>

								
